<!-- Event 2 (supply shortage): accident blocking two of the three motor lanes of
     the busiest LDD-instrumented primary arterial 29119849#1 (53 m, ~97 veh/km
     morning peak) from 08:00 to 09:30 (28800-34200 s).
     docs/5_rq3_event_scenarios.md sec 4.

     Lane topology constrains this. _0 is a pedestrian lane and _1 a bicycle
     lane; of the three motor lanes, _2 and _3 both run straight to 29119850
     (788 and 551 veh in the window) and _4 is left-turn-only to -gneE9 (62 veh).
     Closing _2+_3 would strand all 1,339 through vehicles, so the blockage takes
     _3 and _4 and leaves _2 open: through traffic keeps a route at half
     capacity and queues, rather than being deleted from the network.

     allow="authority" is load-bearing, not decoration. Until 2026-08-08 this
     file used a bare <closingReroute/> with no allow/disallow, which applies no
     permission change at all: the edge stayed fully passable and 1401
     (baseline) vs 1404 (event) vehicles entered it, so the event did nothing.

     Measured against the no-event counterfactual over 07:30-08:30
     (scripts/simulation/smoke_event_closure.py): link density rises to 79.8 from
     65 veh/km and speed falls to 2.1-4.5 m/s, mean network trip duration +5.9 s,
     while wrongLane teleports stay at 31 against 13 no-event. A full closure of
     all three motor lanes was tried first and rejected: it stranded the half of
     the background traffic that carries no rerouting device, taking wrongLane
     teleports to 84, and produced LESS network delay (+2.9 s) because it removed
     the traffic instead of queueing it.

     Pinned by tests/routing/test_event_injection.py. -->
<additional>
  <rerouter id="accident_0704" edges="29119849#1">
    <interval begin="28800" end="34200">
      <closingLaneReroute id="29119849#1_3" allow="authority"/>
      <closingLaneReroute id="29119849#1_4" allow="authority"/>
    </interval>
  </rerouter>
</additional>
